{%- comment -%}
  Responsive image with srcset per the performance brief (§6).
  Params:
    image  — image object (required)
    sizes  — sizes attribute (default '100vw')
    eager  — true only above the fold; everything else lazy-loads
    class  — extra classes
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign sizes = sizes | default: '100vw'
  assign loading = 'lazy'
  assign fetchpriority = 'auto'
  if eager
    assign loading = 'eager'
    assign fetchpriority = 'high'
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The candidate list runs past 1600 on purpose. A desktop slot is served
  at CSS width x device pixel ratio, so a 640px card on a 2x display
  needs 1280 real pixels and a full-bleed image on a 2x 1440 monitor
  needs 2880 - a 1600 ceiling meant the browser had nothing sharp enough
  to pick and upscaled the largest candidate, which is what made desktop
  images look soft. Shopify clamps each candidate to the source file's
  real dimensions, so listing large widths never invents pixels or
  serves an upscaled file; it only stops us capping below what the
  source can actually give.

  This puts the burden on `sizes` being accurate: with a wrong 100vw the
  browser would now fetch a 3000px file for a 560px card. Every caller
  that renders into a fixed-width slot passes its real width.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{
  image
  | image_url: width: 3000
  | image_tag:
    widths: '360, 540, 720, 900, 1080, 1440, 1800, 2200, 2600, 3000',
    sizes: sizes,
    loading: loading,
    fetchpriority: fetchpriority,
    decoding: 'async',
    class: class
}}
